BAGHDAD — On 31 October 2010, a congregation gathered for Sunday Mass at Our Lady of Salvation Church in Baghdad’s Karrada district. The evening was supposed to be an ordinary expression of faith — prayers, familiar rituals, and the gathering of a community whose roots in Beth Nahrin (Mesopotamia) long predated the modern Iraqi state. &hellip;
Instead, it became one of the deadliest attacks on Christians in Iraq’s recent history.
Armed militants stormed the Syriac Catholic church, took worshippers hostage, and opened fire. When Iraqi security forces launched an operation to free the congregation, the attackers detonated explosives inside the building. By the end of the siege, dozens of worshippers and security personnel were dead. Among the victims were two priests, Father Thaer Abdul and Father Wasim Al-Qass Botros, who had been celebrating Mass.

