DARAMSUQ (DAMASCUS) — Iraqi Prime Minister Ali al-Zaidi departed for Daramsuq (Damascus) at the head of a high-level delegation to meet with Syrian President Ahmed al-Sharaa and discuss ways to strengthen bilateral relations, particularly in the fields of trade, oil, energy and security. The visit reflects Baghdad’s efforts to expand cooperation with Syria’s new administration. &hellip;
The visit comes as the two countries seek to develop relations following the fall of Bashar al-Assad’s regime in December 2024. Both face shared challenges involving trade, security, their more than 600-kilometer border, and the fight against the terrorist Islamic State (ISIS). These issues make security and economic coordination a strategic priority for both sides.
