SHIGUR (SINJAR), Iraq — Iraq began transferring strategic military positions on Mount Shigur (Sinjar) to federal forces on 26 October, as the Sinjar Resistance Units (YBS) agreed to end their independent military structure and integrate their members into the country’s official security institutions. The move, overseen by Prime Minister and Commander-in-Chief Ali al-Zaidi, is intended &hellip;
Sabah al-Numan, spokesman for the commander-in-chief of the Iraqi armed forces, said the process was part of the government’s efforts to organize security responsibilities under federal authority and ensure that weapons remain under state control.
According to Iraqi security officials, specialized committees will oversee the integration of YBS fighters into official institutions. The group has put its combined strength, including the Sinjar Women’s Units , at approximately 4,165 fighters, although the process is expected to proceed in phases.
